VGE-owned HDR stars and galaxy
=============================

Status: Phases 1 through 4 complete after second review and independent completion audit (2026-10-13). Appearance/performance acceptance remains open in Phase 5.
Authority: User-approved hybrid HDR direction and refinements (2026-10-13).
Related plan: PBR.PhotometricLighting.todo, shared nighttime illumination work.
Diagnostic evidence: ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md.
Linked contracts: PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md (calibrated nighttime celestial
lighting) and PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md (publication and draw ownership).

Goal and approved constraints
  Replace the native stars/galaxy draw with VGE-owned rendering and shader code.
  Generate a linear HDR galaxy cubemap with a VGE shader during world loading;
  do not reuse the base-game galaxy cubemap or its shader implementation.
  Render individual stars using instanced billboards, limited to a few thousand,
  with the count controlled by the global sky quality level.
  Use a common calibrated celestial source for visible appearance and illumination.
  Reuse atmosphere, GPU resource/publication, storage, exposure and glare owners.
  Preserve the current shader baseline. No millions-of-stars compute rasterizer,
  volumetric galaxy simulation, additional shadow maps or block-light changes.

Research references
  - Smaller than Pixels: Rendering Millions of Stars in Real-Time (2025):
    https://elib.dlr.de/215930/1/egs20251029.pdf
    Reference for angular-area normalization and stable subpixel star filtering;
    its atomic compute rasterizer is not the selected implementation.
  - A Physically-Based Nightsky Model (2001):
    https://graphics.stanford.edu/papers/nightsky/nightsky.pdf
    Reference for consistent source appearance, illumination and atmospheric transport.

Phase 1: Define source, quality and publication contracts
-------------------------------------------------------
☑ Identify the authoritative global sky quality option and its supported levels.
  - Reconcile the existing Atmosphere.SkyLutQuality option with the requested global
    setting before wiring consumers; do not introduce an independent star-quality knob.
  - Record exact per-level star caps, cubemap resolutions and generation budgets.
    Keep the highest cap within a few thousand and lower levels strict subsets.
☑ Specify deterministic star and procedural galaxy inputs.
  - Choose a compact licensed catalog or deterministic generated distribution;
    document direction, magnitude, linear color and stable seed/orientation conventions.
  - Define the galaxy shader's broad band, bulge, dust attenuation and multiscale
    structure. Treat this as an artistic spatial model with calibrated light output,
    not a measured reconstruction of the Milky Way.
  - Split resolved stars from unresolved background without duplicate energy. Account
    for stars omitted at lower quality so quality changes do not change total illumination.
☑ Define physical units, normalization, HDR encoding and conservative source bounds.
  - Convert star magnitudes to integrated photometric contributions and normalize
    their filtered angular footprints. Define the diffuse map's luminance budget.
  - Keep airglow separate; replace only the corresponding existing stellar background
    contribution. Preserve moonlight and avoid adding the old background a second time.
  - Specify scaled floating-point cubemap storage, mip filtering and precision checks.
    Do not apply legacy black subtraction, display-space processing or the generic
    compatibility brightness conversion to generated celestial radiance.
☑ Specify complete publication and failure behavior with existing resource owners.
  - Galaxy generation is a world-loading operation, not per-frame work. Rebuild only
    for relevant quality/source changes, context recovery or shader reload as required.
  - Retain the last complete publication on failed replacement; define first-load
    failure handling without reactivating the native stars/galaxy renderer.

Phase 2: Generate the HDR galaxy during world loading
---------------------------------------------------
☑ Implement a VGE-owned GPU generation program for all six cubemap faces.
  - Generate galaxy structure directly from the agreed deterministic inputs. Do not
    sample native sky textures or copy native shader formulas.
  - Use continuous directional coordinates, filtered procedural detail and consistent
    edge treatment to avoid face seams and aliasing in fine dust/stellar structure.
☑ Allocate the agreed floating-point map, generate seam-consistent linear mips and
  produce the derived lighting representation before publishing the complete resource set.
  - Respect GPU context/thread ownership; bound loading cost, scratch memory and work.
  - Keep resources stable across ordinary frames and release them on world teardown.
☑ Validate deterministic generation, finite nonnegative values, normalization, seams,
  mip energy and preservation of faint gradients through the actual texture format.

Phase 3: Render the instanced stars and owned galaxy
-------------------------------------------------
☑ Upload the bounded star set to a persistent instance buffer and draw instanced quads.
  - Select nested quality subsets deterministically; no per-frame catalog rebuild/upload.
  - Use a normalized footprint with stable subpixel movement. Verify integrated light
    across resolution, field of view, screen position and overlapping star footprints.
  - Define edge clipping and culling conservatively so visible footprints do not pop.
☑ Sample the generated galaxy with a VGE-owned shader and matching sky orientation.
  - Preserve daily celestial rotation and horizon/terrain occlusion through existing
    frame inputs. Compose behind atmosphere, clouds and celestial disks correctly.
☑ Register the owned stars/galaxy renderer and disable the native night draw.
  - Register VGE's renderer independently and unregister the native draw without a Harmony
    callback patch; remove obsolete compatibility patching
    for this draw. Avoid duplicate submissions, broad state invalidation or version bumps.
    Preserve the native client-system registry and asset lifecycle.
  - Preserve state tracking, reload behavior and lifecycle disposal contracts.
☑ Route both contributions through existing frame storage, exposure and glare.
  - Apply atmospheric transmittance consistently and only once. Let atmospheric
    radiance and exposure govern daylight visibility without a legacy artistic cutoff.
  - Filter the star core without a second independently boosted halo. Evaluate existing
    glare behavior before changing it; document any further required scope separately.

Phase 4: Reconcile celestial appearance and scene illumination
-----------------------------------------------------------
☑ Derive a cached diffuse-light representation from the same complete celestial source.
  - Include the full source budget regardless of the displayed star subset. Integrate
    with solid-angle and cosine weights; do not infer illuminance from peak texel values.
  - Rotate and attenuate this source consistently with visible sky, using existing
    atmosphere transport/publication owners and appropriate refresh dependencies.
☑ Replace the matching constant stellar background in AtmosphereNightLighting.
  - Keep separately modeled airglow and lunar contributions explicit. Reconcile weather
    attenuation and any approximations so they are neither omitted nor applied twice.
☑ Publish coherent results to standalone PBR and LumOn consumers.
  - Cover terrain, forward items/entities, liquids, reflection/environment sampling,
    water transport and probe/cache consumers where applicable.
  - Preserve interior visibility and AO; update lighting bounds, source identities and
    invalidation dependencies. Do not brighten terrain through emission or arbitrary floors.

Phase 5: Qualification and appearance acceptance
-----------------------------------------------
☐ Run focused CPU/GPU checks through subagents, keeping routine fixtures lightweight.
  - Verify star flux and color, footprint stability, quality-budget invariance, galaxy
    continuity, source-to-lighting integration and transmittance consistency.
  - Verify no overflow or loss of faint gradients at actual storage boundaries, including
    simultaneous daylight/twilight sources and exposure extremes.
  - Exercise world load/unload, failed generation, reload, resize, quality changes and
    complete publication without stale textures or duplicate native rendering.
☐ Measure generation latency, resident/scratch memory and steady-state frame cost.
  - Record hardware, resolution and quality; separate loading cost from runtime cost.
    Confirm galaxy generation does not repeat during ordinary frames. Keep expensive
    timing/capture qualification opt-in rather than part of routine correctness tests.
☐ Obtain user-run appearance acceptance.
  - Check fixed exposure first, then auto exposure: moonless/moonlit nights, twilight,
    lantern comparisons, galaxy gradients, star movement, FOV/resolution changes,
    all quality levels, clouds/horizon, terrain/items/water and LumOn on/off.
  - Assess exposure readability separately from source calibration; do not conceal
    source mismatch by increasing ambient brightness or glare.
☐ Reconcile photometric and atmosphere documentation and independently review completion.
  - Record calibration choices, procedural approximations, quality caps, resources,
    validation receipts and actual visual coverage before marking the work complete.

Completion criterion
  VGE owns star/galaxy resources, generation, shaders and drawing without native sky
  shader code or galaxy imagery. A bounded instanced star set and load-generated HDR
  galaxy share a qualified lighting calibration, preserve smooth gradients and stable
  star energy, meet recorded performance budgets and receive user appearance acceptance.

Resolved source, quality and publication contract (2026-10-13)
-------------------------------------------------------------
This section governs implementation. Generation receipts below qualify the source resource,
not its visible draw or activation as scene illumination.

Quality and bounded work
  Use Atmosphere.SkyLutQuality as the single VGE sky quality setting: integer 0..3,
  default 0, sanitized by AtmosphereSettings.Sanitize. Keep its JSON key and existing
  atmosphere LUT scaling; update its UI label/tooltip to describe global sky quality
  when wiring the new consumers. No second selector or config migration is needed.
  Confirmed in AtmosphereSettings.cs and configlib-patches.json.

  Level   Instanced stars   Galaxy face size   Max noise octaves   Cubemap with mips
    0          1024              512                  4                 8 MiB
    1          2048             1536                  5                72 MiB
    2          3072             2048                  6               128 MiB
    3          4096             2048                  7               128 MiB

  Memory figures are rounded up for six R11G11B10F faces and a complete mip chain.
  Allow two final-format maps during replacement (retained and candidate), plus one
  RGBA16F scratch map and 8 MiB for source buffers, reduction and lighting data: 40/296/520/520 MiB.
  Implementation uses a single 128x128 RGBA32F scratch tile instead of that full scratch
  map, retaining float32 arithmetic until explicit packed-format narrowing. It remains
  within these conservative budgets and does not change scene framebuffer formats.
  Resolution revision (2026-10-13): user selected 512 pixels per face for the lowest
  level and 2048 for the highest. Use 512/1536/2048/2048; these replace
  the sizes reviewed originally. Highest-quality resident map storage is bounded by
  128 MiB; the conservative replacement budget is 520 MiB. Reuse/tile scratch where
  possible during implementation; do not assume a full-size scratch map is necessary.
  Qualify allocation support (including non-power-of-two faces and partial edge tiles), peak memory
  and loading time at these revised sizes before accepting the implementation.
  Rasterize six faces with a GLSL 330 generation program through existing resource APIs;
  do not require a new compute capability. Generate at most 128x128 texels per submitted
  tile, seven bounded noise octaves, then bounded mip/reduction passes. Never spin-wait
  on completion. Schedule on the existing render-context owner during world loading.
  Qualification targets: <=1 second aggregate generation GPU time at highest quality,
  <=0.5 ms steady-state at 2560x1440 on the qualification machine. Record that machine;
  these are acceptance targets, not measurements or promises for every GPU. Do not
  silently reduce selected quality on failure. Resize/exposure changes never regenerate.

Deterministic source and orientation
  Choose a generated fictional sky, avoiding external catalog licensing and native assets.
  Source version 1 has exactly 4096 stable IDs, a fixed seed 0x56474531, and independent
  unsigned integer hash streams keyed by (version, ID, purpose); specify the exact hash
  implementation with fixtures when implementing. No world RNG or frame seed is consumed.
  Distribute directions uniformly over the unit sphere using z=1-2u, azimuth=2*pi*v.
  Assign raw magnitude m=-1+8*u^(1/3); sort by increasing magnitude, breaking ties by ID.
  Every quality level selects a prefix of this same list. Use a separate random stream
  to interpolate a bounded warm/neutral/cool linear RGB palette; normalize Rec.709 Y to
  one. Palette endpoints before normalization: (1,.65,.35), (1,1,1), (.55,.70,1).
  This is a controlled artistic distribution, not a real star catalog or spectral model.

  Store directions and the cubemap in one right-handed celestial frame (+Y pole,
  +X zero longitude, +Z positive longitude). Define the procedural galaxy plane tilted
  60 degrees about +X, with its bulge at galaxy longitude zero. Use a normalized
  celestial-to-world rotation derived from calendar time and latitude for both stars
  and map; derive inverse sampling from that same matrix. Do not read matrices produced
  as a side effect of the suppressed callback. Verify installed calendar axes/time
  conventions against fixed reference directions during draw integration. World position
  translation never enters this rotation; no galaxy parallax or per-world seed changes.

  Galaxy shape is positive: a broad Gaussian latitude band (sigma 12 degrees), a
  localized bulge (longitude sigma 25 degrees, latitude sigma 8 degrees), attenuated
  by bounded procedural dust, with direction-space multiscale value noise. Define
  W=.05 + (band + 2*bulge)*D*M, D in [exp(-2),1], M in [.5,1.5]; hence .05<=W<=4.55.
  Band and bulge are each in [0,1]. Evaluate continuous unit directions across faces;
  attenuate noise frequencies beyond the pixel footprint. No native texture lookup,
  photographic input, hard black subtraction or thresholded display-space gradients.
  Normalize a warm-neutral bounded color field to Y=1 using the same palette envelope.

Energy partition and atmospheric treatment
  Retain 0.0008 lux as the chosen clear-reference, orientation-averaged horizontal
  moonless total. Allocate 0.0004 to neutral airglow, 0.0003 to unresolved galaxy and
  0.0001 to the full discrete star set. This split is an explicit VGE artistic calibration,
  not a measured astronomical breakdown or a claim from the papers. No additional floor.
  Reference altitude is 0.001 km, aerosol 1, cloud cover zero. Normalize celestial source
  amplitudes once against the existing transport at this reference, averaged over uniform
  rotations; do not renormalize each night, current orientation, weather or exposure.
  Actual directional sky brightness and horizontal illuminance are allowed to vary.

  For a star, raw integrated illuminance E_i=E0*10^(-0.4*m_i), where
  E0=108000/(206264.806247^2) lux follows the 2025 paper's magnitude/area convention.
  A single shared positive scale brings the complete star set to its declared reference
  budget. Store the resulting E_i and equivalent calibrated magnitude explicitly;
  raw magnitude controls relative brightness and does not claim Earth calibration.
  Normalize the galaxy independently against its declared budget. Reference transport
  responses must be finite and positive; otherwise reject generation, never divide by zero.
  Airglow remains the existing uniform upper-hemisphere approximation at .0004/pi nits,
  already ground-level; do not extinguish it as an extraterrestrial source a second time.

  Visible star pixel radiance is E_i*w_p/Omega_p, with nonnegative footprint weights
  summing to one over the full unoccluded footprint and pixel solid angle Omega_p.
  Use a minimum two-pixel-wide filtered core; retain full-footprint normalization at
  screen/terrain edges (do not renormalize the surviving clipped pixels). Normalize
  before atmospheric transmittance and visibility, which physically reduce received flux.
  Instanced quads use additive linear RGB and no independent brightness-scaled halo.

  The base galaxy has no discrete catalog contribution. For low quality, splat only
  omitted stars into the generated background with a normalized spherical kernel;
  remove exactly those IDs from the instance draw. Keep RGB energy and direction in
  the residual, rather than adding a uniform brightness floor. Use a fixed 2-degree
  kernel, integrated over texel solid angles, with explicit cross-face support.
  Lighting integrates the base galaxy plus all 4096 stars exactly once, independently
  of the display split. Use a quality-independent 64x64-per-face source quadrature
  and analytic star directions through existing atmospheric transport. Cached lighting
  is approximate spatially; target <=1% integrated RGB error against refined quadrature.
  Display residuals and map resolution must preserve source integrated RGB within 1%.

  Apply atmosphere transmission once to visible external sources and to their incident
  environment integral. Reuse existing medium data and publication scheduling, not a
  second atmospheric integrator. Retain the bounded cloud factor 1-.9*coverage once
  for background illumination; reconcile the visible cloud path to avoid applying its
  occlusion twice. Finite-path background scattering remains the documented isotropic
  approximation derived from the same attenuated environment E/pi, not a separate source.
  Remove the previous combined .0008/pi background when activating this complete source;
  retain lunar transport and sun/moon shadow handover unchanged.

HDR representation, bounds and qualification obligations
  Generate and normalize with float32 arithmetic. Store the galaxy plus omitted-star
  residual as R11G11B10F (GL_R11F_G11F_B10F), RGB = physical nits * C, with no alpha.
  This is the user-selected initial format. C is a fixed power
  of two per completed source generation, independent of camera exposure. Choose the
  largest such C with C*B<=32000 for the proven per-channel map bound B; decode once.
  This new resource's own encoding does not change existing atmosphere's fixed 1/16 scale.
  Derive B conservatively from W<=4.55, palette maxima, positive normalization factors
  and the sum of omitted-star E_i times the residual kernel's maximum density. Mips are
  nonnegative solid-angle-weighted averages and cannot increase B. Retain safety clamping
  at storage boundaries, but reject an out-of-domain source instead of relying on clipping.

  Bound billboard radiance by sum(E_i*maxRGB_i)/minimum admitted pixel solid angle;
  derive that minimum from the actual projection/viewport and filtered footprint. Include
  overlap of all admitted stars, galaxy maximum and existing celestial/atmospheric terms
  in frame-lighting admission. A zero/invalid angular area rejects the draw. Never reuse
  the galaxy map bound for star peaks. Use existing defensive scene-storage conversion.
  Required later tests: no NaN/Inf, <=1% integrated energy drift, <=1% red/green and <=2% blue storage error
  for positive map samples >=1e-5 of peak, and absolute error <=1e-7 of peak below that.
  Failure of these criteria is a storage-design issue to resolve before release; no
  broad framebuffer-format or GLSL-version change is authorized by this contract.

Publication and recovery
  AtmosphereModSystem remains the scheduling/composition owner. Separate celestial source
  generation, resource storage and instanced drawing responsibilities within that domain.
  Publish map/mips, instance subset, source quadrature, encoding, conservative bounds and
  source identity as one immutable completed source set. Source identity includes algorithm
  version, seed, quality and calibration. Atmospheric results also identify the source set,
  rotation and admitted medium; commit visible and lighting consumers together at the
  existing pre-scene publication boundary. A pending rotation/medium result retains its
  matching completed source/rotation; never pair new stars with stale illumination.
  Quality changes rebuild off to the side. Reload invalidates pending work; retain valid
  completed resources until replacement succeeds. Context loss invalidates all GPU handles
  and returns to startup behavior. Generation/upload errors discard only the candidate.
  Before first success, supply zero stellar/galactic contribution and no owned star draw;
  keep solar/lunar and the separately defined airglow valid. Suppress native stars for the
  owned renderer lifetime, including failure; log failure and retry only on reload/reset
  or relevant input change. Do not silently fall back to native assets or mismatched light.
  Retire old resources only after outstanding GPU use is safe. World unload cancels work,
  clears publication and disposes resources on the owning context. No per-frame readback.

Phase 1 verification boundary
  This is a design-contract phase. GPU generation, exact hash fixtures, native calendar
  adapter verification, numeric tolerances, timing and appearance are obligations of the
  subsequent implementation tasks, not measurements established by these decisions.
  Traceability: quality -> AtmosphereSettings.cs/configlib-patches.json; source/units ->
  cited 2025 paper section 3.2 and 2001 paper source decomposition; diagnostics ->
  ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md; transport/publication ->
  PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md, calibrated nighttime celestial lighting, and
  PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md, publication/draw ownership. The selected split and
  procedural shape are VGE design decisions, not inferred numerical results from papers.

Generation implementation and verification (2026-10-13)
------------------------------------------------------
  Source owners: PBR/Atmosphere/Celestial/CelestialSource.cs, CelestialProceduralField.cs,
  CelestialResidual.cs and CelestialCubeGeometry.cs. Unsigned hash uses xor-shifts
  16/15/16 and wrapping multipliers 0x7feb352d/0x846ca68b. CPU/GPU fixtures lock the
  implementation. Calibration reuses AtmosphereModel.ExternalTransmittance at the
  declared reference; its uniform-rotation response is integrated once per source build.
  The full catalog and 64x64-per-face quadrature remain quality independent. Residual
  two-degree caps normalize discrete solid-angle RGB across every intersected face.

  GPU owners: CelestialGenerationProgram, CelestialGalaxyGeneration, the reusable
  Rendering/DynamicTextureCube and CelestialResourceSet. GLSL 330 rasterizes into a separate 128x128 float32 scratch
  tile; copies populate all six R11G11B10F faces. Mips use positive solid-angle child
  weights, including proportional 3-to-1 reduction for the 1536 chain. At fine levels
  a positive midpoint Jacobian avoids FP32 corner-primitive cancellation; coarse levels
  use the analytic area. Explicit nearest rounding qualifies the packed conversion.
  No native sky image, shader formula, compute capability or shader-version increase.

  AtmosphereModSystem composes CelestialSourceLifetime. BlockTexturesLoaded prepares
  deterministic inputs on a worker; LevelFinalize submits GPU generation on the owning
  context before returning, using the existing uniform allocator or a private loading
  epoch. Ordinary frames only poll the completion fence or a relevant changed quality.
  No GPU spin-wait, readback or repeated unchanged generation. Publication exposes the
  complete cube/mips and matching immutable source, encoding, bounds and quadrature.
  Reload failure retains the old complete set; world teardown cancels preparation and
  clears resources. Context-generation changes withdraw dead handles and rebuild.
  Generation prepares the complete source. Runtime activation now waits for its matching
  scene-light integration, described below; generation alone does not alter receiver lighting.

  Receipts: ../artifacts/celestial-lifecycle-regression.log (50 passed, one explicit
  full-size opt-in skip) and ../artifacts/celestial-qualification-double.log (9 passed,
  including four full-size qualities). Production/test SPIR-V builds passed. Checks
  include deterministic CPU/GPU output, source reference budgets, refined quadrature,
  directional continuity, finite/nonnegative actual packed faces, <=1% integrated RGB
  map/mip drift, packed channel precision, loading without a frame allocator, unchanged
  frames, failed reload retention and retirement. Full-size qualification uses double
  summation so the diagnostic does not lose precision across millions of texels.
  Final rebuilt-binary checks: ../artifacts/celestial-final-default.log (10 passed,
  one full-size opt-in skip), including the final cleanup and shader documentation.
  Second review and independent completion audit found no remaining required-now issues.

  Qualification machine: NVIDIA RTX 4090, OpenGL 4.3, driver 591.86. Levels 0..3:
  GPU generation 55.81/193.15/285.83/298.76 ms; preparation-through-completion wall time
  1430.11/5169.68/5284.08/457.45 ms; packed map storage 8/72/128/128 MiB. GPU timing
  passes the selected one-second highest-quality target. Wall time includes CPU source
  and residual preparation, executable preparation and submission; it is not GPU time.
  Allocation payload accounting: two retained/candidate maps plus one 0.25-MiB tile,
  catalog/index textures and at most a 2-MiB loading uniform page fit the specified
  conservative replacement budgets. Driver allocator overhead is not measured here.
  Full-size measurement remains opt-in via VGE_RUN_CELESTIAL_QUALIFICATION=1.
  Runtime draw timing, live appearance and source-to-scene transport remain later work.

Owned drawing implementation and verification (2026-10-13)
---------------------------------------------------------
  CelestialResourceSet owns an immutable CelestialStarGeometry instance stream and
  its generated map. The subset uploads once per source, not per frame; six procedural
  vertices per instance produce bounded screen-space billboards. Analytically integrated
  compact tent weights sum to one over the unoccluded footprint, including subpixel
  movement. A two-pixel conservative raster padding retains partially visible pixels;
  clipping does not renormalize surviving energy. Per-pixel perspective angular area
  converts calibrated integrated lux into physical RGB nits. No second halo is added.

  CelestialOrientation derives both directions and inverse map sampling from the same
  calendar transform, independent of suppressed callback side effects or translation.
  Installed calendar evidence: ../artifacts/phase4-late-owner-il.txt, native night-sky
  callback, TotalDays minus YearRel modulo one, latitude-dependent tilted rotation axis.
  Fixed-direction, proper-rotation and negative-date fixtures qualify that adapter.

  CelestialRenderer runs at Opaque order 0.19 before the atmosphere at 0.2. Typed GLSL
  330 star/galaxy programs use existing graphics command/state owners, primary RGB HDR,
  frozen frame storage, camera exposure and scene-image glare. VGE registers its own
  renderer independently and unregisters the native night draw through the public event
  manager, leaving the native system and its mesh/texture lifecycle intact.
  No Harmony callback patch remains. Failed preparation never reinstalls native drawing.
  Missing initial
  source skips owned drawing. Reset/reload/world teardown retire owned resources;
  context-generation changes also recreate procedural geometry rather than retaining a
  dead VAO. Generation replacement failures retain the previous complete source.

  The atmosphere producer owns the float32 dimensionless external-transmission table.
  Its existing sky compute dispatch generates all 512 elevations alongside sky/aerial
  transport and publishes them together after the same fence. CPU fallback builds and
  caches the table on the atmosphere worker using AtmosphereModel.ExternalTransmittance.
  CelestialRenderer borrows the published texture; drawing never integrates atmosphere.
  Both sources attenuate once.
  The atmosphere retains local horizon/flat-fog and underwater background occlusion,
  with full atmospheric RGB; legacy daylight and cloud-proxy fades are bypassed. Actual
  clouds render later. Its old uniform stellar half is removed from the visible sky
  using matching LUT row interpolation, leaving the separately budgeted airglow half.
  At the Phase 3 receipt, receiver environment/aerial lighting retained the prior approximation.
  The Phase 4 integration below supersedes this intermediate state.

  CelestialDrawAdmission bounds every admitted star's possible overlap using the actual
  rigid perspective camera and minimum pixel angular area, plus the map maximum and
  arithmetic headroom. The immutable source precomputes the displayed subset's sum
  of maximum RGB flux during calibration; frame admission only recalculates camera
  angular area, not a per-star loop. Unsupported projections/invalid areas reject drawing. Scene
  lighting publication includes this envelope before freezing storage; the retired
  native star-image envelope is excluded while owned. Existing defensive storage
  conversion remains in place. Resize/exposure never regenerate the source catalog/map.

  Traceability: instancing/flux -> resolved contract and cited 2025 paper section 3.2;
  orientation -> installed calendar receipt above; composition/ownership/lifecycle ->
  PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md, Draw ownership; physical routing and intermediate
  receiver boundary -> PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md, Calibrated nighttime
  celestial lighting; original artifacts -> ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md.
  Existing bloom reads scene RGB; no independent stellar glare gain/filter was added.
  Runtime glare appearance and any proposed changes remain separate acceptance work.

  Receipt: ../artifacts/celestial-draw-tests.log, fresh shader-enabled build and 101
  passing tests, one explicit full-size generation qualification skip, zero failures.
  Actual production GPU draws qualify <1% RGB flux drift with independent exact pixel
  solid-angle integration across tested resolutions/FOV/positions, overlapping stars,
  screen clipping, rear-facing rejection, per-channel attenuation, map decode/rotation,
  and unchanged glow output. Off-row transmission matches existing transport in four
  clear/cloudy ground/elevated media. Coverage includes persistent instance prefixes,
  draw-range admission, installed native suppression, reload/resize/world retirement,
  tracked state restoration and retained atmosphere/airglow without legacy daylight fade.
  Second review and independent completion audit found no required-now issues.
  Full-resolution runtime timing and live user appearance remain Phase 5, unmeasured here.

Renderer registration correction (2026-10-13)
--------------------------------------------
  VGE's CelestialRenderer registers independently at Opaque order 0.19, before the
  atmospheric renderer at 0.2. NativeNightSkyDrawRegistration disables only the native
  OnRenderFrame3D renderer wrappers through the public event manager. It leaves the
  clientSystems array, native system instance, asset callbacks, mesh and textures intact.
  Repeated disable calls are harmless and unrelated render callbacks are preserved.
  Reload, resize and missing owned resources never reactivate native drawing.

  The RenderNightSky ClientSystem adapter and its slot replacement/native disposal
  were removed. CelestialNightDrawHook remains removed; no Harmony draw interception
  is used. Engine and mod lifecycles retain their existing resource owners.
  Installed public-registry evidence remains ../artifacts/NightSystemInspection/results.json
  and lifecycle.txt. The earlier night-system test receipts describe the superseded
  replacement approach and are not validation of this registration correction.
  Correction receipts: ../artifacts/night-draw-registration-build.log and
  ../artifacts/night-draw-registration-tests.log. Fresh shader-enabled build passed;
  54 focused tests passed, one full-size opt-in skip, zero failures. Installed-native
  registration fixtures verify unchanged system-array/instance identity, exact draw
  removal, unrelated callback preservation, idempotence and no native resource disposal.
  Existing GPU generation, celestial draw/flux, transmission, sky composition and
  owned lifecycle checks remain passing.

Cached flux and transmission cost (2026-10-13)
---------------------------------------------
  CelestialSource.ResolvedStarMaximumFluxLux retains the displayed prefix's maximum
  RGB flux sum, preserving the previous float32 products and double accumulation order.
  All four quality-prefix fixtures establish exact equality with independent summation.
  Receipt: ../artifacts/celestial-flux-precompute-tests.log, fresh shader-enabled build,
  31 focused tests passed, no skips or failures.

  Prior CPU transmission work was 512 elevation rays with at most 12 extinction
  samples each, not full radiance/multiple-scattering integration. It refreshes only
  for completed altitude/weather changes or resource retirement. An i9-12900K Debug
  headless measurement at .001/1 km and cloud coverage 0/.75 found median integration
  0.311..0.312 ms, complete Update CPU wall time 0.317..0.319 ms and unchanged cached
  calls approximately 0.018 microseconds average. Ten warmed rebuilds and 100,000
  unchanged calls; RTX 4090, OpenGL 4.3, NVIDIA 591.86. Upload submission is included,
  GPU execution time is not measured. Receipt: ../artifacts/celestial-transmission-cost.log.
  These are diagnostic CPU timings, not live frame-cost or appearance acceptance.

Shared atmospheric transmission production (2026-10-13)
------------------------------------------------------
  The existing atmosphere_sky compute dispatch now writes 512 external-transmission
  records in the same output buffer as sky/aerial transport. Minimum dispatch coverage
  includes all 512 rows even when the sky grid has only 16x8 cells. The existing output
  fence/readback and immutable AtmosphereLighting snapshot carry the complete table;
  no separate compute program, dispatch, fence or synchronous readback was introduced.
  AtmosphereTextureSet owns its 8-KiB RGBA32F texture and validates/uploads it before
  AtmosphereModSystem swaps the complete publication. Empty startup snapshots use
  one zero-RGB texel. Invalid transmission retains the prior entire generation.

  CPU fallback uses AtmosphereExternalTransmission.Build on the existing atmosphere
  worker and caches by admitted altitude/aerosol. CelestialTransmission was removed;
  CelestialRenderer only borrows AtmosphereModSystem.ExternalTransmissionTexture.
  Solar/lunar composition retains the solar snapshot's matching medium table without
  scaling or adding dimensionless extinction. The radial model is azimuth independent,
  so 512 horizon-focused elevation samples describe this function at the admitted
  observer altitude/weather. Sky radiance additionally varies with source-relative
  azimuth and uses its separate 2D smooth/Mie representation.

  Receipts: ../artifacts/atmosphere-external-transmission-build.log and
  ../artifacts/atmosphere-external-transmission-focused.log. Shader-enabled build passed;
  62 focused tests passed, no skips/failures. Coverage includes all 512 rows against CPU
  across six atmospheric cases, minimum-size dispatch, dimensionless actual uploads,
  invalid-data retention, CPU fallback, paired celestial transport, reload/reset resource
  ownership and actual star-shader interpolation.
  Broader discovery reported four unresolved fixture failures outside these changed
  paths: two sky integration fixtures missing frozen storage, a solar display storage
  binding contract and a LumOn debug sampler-count assertion. Baseline reproduction
  was not performed; these are broader validation limitations, not established regressions
  or established pre-existing defects. No runtime timing improvement is claimed here.

Source-derived scene illumination implementation (2026-10-13)
-----------------------------------------------------------
  Phase 4 traceability: diffuse source/budget/weights -> resolved Energy partition and
  atmospheric treatment contract above; source-coherent publication and lifecycle ->
  PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md, Publication contract and Draw ownership; airglow/lunar
  separation, finite-path approximation, visibility/AO and existing consumer bindings ->
  PBR.PhotometricLighting.Proposal.md, Calibrated nighttime celestial lighting, and
  PBR.PhotometricLighting.todo, shared nighttime illumination work. The original capture
  findings in ../artifacts/LightingImbalance/findings.md establish the calibration mismatch,
  not validation of the replacement. The 2001 paper's source decomposition informs the
  appearance/illumination distinction; VGE's resolved numerical source contract governs
  this implementation rather than copying its uniform stellar illumination approximation.

  CelestialDiffuseLighting sums the fixed 64x64-per-face base galaxy quadrature and every
  catalog star, through the completed external-transmission table and frozen celestial
  rotation. Double accumulation with solid-angle and horizontal cosine weights yields RGB
  E/pi. Display residuals and quality prefixes never enter this integral. Cloud proxy enters
  incident light once; visible external sources receive actual later cloud occlusion instead.
  Airglow is separately 0.0004/pi nits with its existing cloud proxy, without external extinction.
  The prior combined 0.0008/pi term and temporary draw-time stellar-half subtraction are removed.
  Actual orientation/weather can change illuminance; reference calibration is never renormalized.

  AtmosphereBackend runs the source sum and finite-path E/pi*(1-T) approximation on a
  cancellable worker after completing shared solar/lunar transport. Rotation-only work
  reuses directional transport and the combined base snapshot. Exact source, rotation,
  medium/grid and celestial inputs identify cached results. No second extinction integrator,
  dispatch, texture, source rebuild or readback is introduced. Horizon compatibility adds the
  same isotropic celestial RGB; directional galaxy scattering is not simulated.

  CelestialSourceLifetime holds a fenced Ready set until successful atmosphere upload.
  AtmosphereModSystem retains failed-upload candidates for retry and commits matching source
  resources at the same pre-scene boundary. CelestialRenderer uses that publication's rotation
  and rejects unmatched source references. Pending/failed work retains the previous complete
  display/light pair; startup has zero external source while airglow/solar/lunar remain valid.
  Reload cancels pending transport, context-invalidated source results cannot activate, and
  teardown cancels managed workers and retires both complete and candidate GPU resources.
  Atmospheric context-generation changes also withdraw old texture handles and pending
  snapshots before recreating the existing transport/storage owners; source recovery retains
  its existing load scheduling rather than reverting to native rendering.

  Existing terrain/forward, liquid reflection/water, LumOn uniform/surface/probe and frame-bound
  consumers read the changed Environment or matching aerial volumes. Interior visibility and
  AO remain unchanged. LumOn source replacement versions surface/probe dependencies; ordinary
  directional motion prioritizes bounded direct relighting while preserving temporal probe
  history. Existing environment reflection remains the scalar E/pi approximation, now sourced
  consistently; a new directional reflection representation is outside this work.

  Receipts: ../artifacts/celestial-diffuse-build.log (fresh forced shader-enabled build,
  zero errors) and ../artifacts/celestial-diffuse-final-tests.log (391 passed, six explicit
  opt-in skips, zero failures). Focused numeric checks establish known cosine/solid-angle
  integrals, quality-independent full source power, RGB/cloud attenuation once and <=1%
  per-channel galaxy error against independently refined 128x128-per-face quadrature across
  representative rotations and ground/elevated clear/aerosol media. CPU/GPU backend fixtures
  verify frozen source/rotation/media during pending work, unchanged-input caching and reload.
  Actual GPU fixtures cover airglow-only composition and persistent storage, integrated failed
  source-pair upload retention and valid retry, stale-source rejection, context withdrawal,
  source commit/retirement and LumOn source revision/priority refresh. Existing forward/direct,
  liquid/water, atmosphere, frame-bound and surface-consumer tests passed in the same union.

  Five broader SurfaceLightingRefresh cases remain separate validation limitations: two
  LightChangesRefreshAllResidentsWithoutRecapture rows, ZeroIndirectBudgetPreservesDirectProgressAndLiveBudgetIdentity,
  RemoteResidentContinuesIndirectRefreshAfterAnotherChunkChanges and ExteriorOcclusionCanHideAndRevealTheSameCapture.
  A fresh shader-enabled attribution build with only the new atmosphere admission call removed
  reproduces all five failures and three passing refresh cases. Receipts:
  ../artifacts/celestial-admission-baseline-build.log and ../artifacts/celestial-admission-baseline-tests.log.
  This establishes unchanged failures with original admission scheduling, not a full historical
  checkout baseline. One raw-RGB-32 assertion reads calibrated stored RGB 0.5415039; the existing
  physical/storage conversion is not changed here. These five were explicitly excluded from
  the final union; its three other refresh cases passed. Production was restored and forcibly
  recompiled after attribution to avoid an incremental DLL retaining the temporary baseline.
  The prior missing sky/sun fixture storage bindings and exact debug-sampler assertion were
  corrected and passed; no production shader baseline or source equation was altered for them.

  Second review and independent completion audit found no remaining Phase 4 issues.
  Phase 5 owns live appearance, exposure/readability and performance qualification; headless
  correctness cannot establish them. The corrected-payload retry fixture does not simulate a
  transient driver failure of identical immutable data, and namespace withdrawal simulates the
  remembered generation changing rather than a full graphics-driver context loss.

